ilure)\n- **Option E (CPAP 5 cmH2O):** As per Option A, CPAP does not provide ventilatory support for CO2 clearance. Additionally, 5 cmH2O is low pressure even for CPAP indications\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- eTG Complete (2024-2025) recommends NIV (BiPAP) for COPD exacerbations with respiratory acidosis (pH 7.25-7.35)\n- ANZICS guidelines state NIV should be initiated within 60 minutes of presentation for eligible patients\n- Contraindications to NIV include: inability to protect airway, facial trauma/burns, haemodynamic instability, severe hypoxaemia refractory to supplemental oxygen, or pH <7.25 (relative contraindication - consider ICU/intubation)\n\n**Clinical Pearls:**\n- NIV success predictors: younger age, lower APACHE II score, pH >7.25, rapid improvement in pH/PaCO2 within 1-2 hours\n- Failure indicators: worsening acidosis, declining consciousness (GCS <8), cardiovascular instability - these require intubation\n- Target SpO2 in COPD with NIV: 88-92% to avoid oxygen-induced hypercapnia while maintaining tissue oxygenation",
