    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "This patient has severe obstructive sleep apnoea (OSA) with significant symptoms, high AHI (38 events/hour), and important occupational considerations as a commercial driver. CPAP therapy is the first-line treatment.\n\n**Why CPAP (Option C) is correct:**\n- **Severity classification:** AHI 38/hour indicates severe OSA (mild: AHI 5-15, moderate: 15-30, severe: >30 events/hour)\n- **Symptomatic:** Epworth Sleepiness Scale 16/24 indicates severe daytime sleepiness (normal <10, >15 = severe)\n- **CPAP mechanism:** Provides continuous positive pressure (typically 10-15 cmH2O) acting as pneumatic splint to maintain upper airway patency during sleep, preventing collapse and apnoeic episodes\n- **Evidence base:** Meta-analyses show CPAP reduces AHI by >90%, improves Epworth score by 2-3 points, reduces cardiovascular events (BP reduction 2-3 mmHg), and decreases motor vehicle accident risk by 70%\n- **Occupational requirement:** Austroads guidelines mandate treatment for commercial drivers with OSA (AHI >20). CPAP allows license retention if compliant (>4 hours/night on 70% of nights). Untreated severe OSA would result in license suspension\n- **Compliance:** Average CPAP compliance 4-6 hours/night. Factors improving compliance: patient education, mask fitting, humidification, pressure ramp, regular follow-up\n\n**Why other options are incorrect:**\n- **Option A (Mandibular advancement splint/MAS):** MAS is a second-line therapy for mild-moderate OSA (AHI 5-30) or CPAP intolerance. Efficacy is lower than CPAP (AHI reduction 50% vs 90%). Given severe OSA (AHI 38) and occupational requirements, MAS is insufficient as first-line therapy. May be considered if CPAP fails/intolerant\n- **Option B (UPPP surgery):** Surgical intervention (UPPP, tonsillectomy, maxillomandibular advancement) is reserved for CPAP failure/intolerance after anatomical assessment. Success rates for UPPP are 40-60% (vs >90% for CPAP). Surgery carries perioperative risks and is not first-line. Requires assessment by ENT/maxillofacial surgeon with Friedman staging\n- **Option D (Weight loss only):** While weight loss is beneficial (10% weight loss may reduce AHI by 25-30%), this patient has severe symptomatic OSA requiring immediate treatment. Weight loss takes months and does not address acute risk. CPAP should be initiated immediately, with weight loss as adjunctive therapy. Waiting 6 months risks cardiovascular events and motor vehicle accidents\n- **Option E (BiPAP with oxygen):** BiPAP is not first-line for OSA. It is indicated for OSA with coexistent hypoventilation (obesity hypoventilation syndrome) or central sleep apnoea. This patient has pure obstructive apnoea. Oxygen supplementation alone does not prevent apnoeic episodes and may prolong apnoea duration (reduced arousal threshold)\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- **Austroads Assessing Fitness to Drive:** Commercial drivers with OSA (AHI >20 or Epworth >10) must undergo treatment and demonstrate compliance (CPAP >4 hours/night on 70% of nights) for license retention. Annual review required\n- **Medicare/PBS:** CPAP machines subsidized if AHI >30 or AHI >15 with cardiovascular comorbidities (e.g., hypertension, atrial fibrillation, stroke). Patient meets criteria (AHI 38)\n- **Sleep Health Foundation Australia:** Recommends sleep physician referral for suspected OSA with Epworth >10 or witnessed apnoeas\n\n**Clinical Pearls:**\n- **CPAP titration:** Overnight titration study or auto-CPAP (APAP) to determine optimal pressure (usually 8-15 cmH2O). Target AHI <5 events/hour on treatment\n- **Mask types:** Nasal mask, nasal pillows, or full-face mask (if mouth breathing). Mask leak and discomfort are main causes of non-compliance\n- **Side effects:** Nasal congestion (use heated humidification), skin irritation (adjust mask), aerophagia (reduce pressure), claustrophobia (desensitization, gradual ramp)\n- **Follow-up:** Review at 1 month, then 3-monthly. Download CPAP compliance data. Repeat sleep study if symptoms persist despite apparent compliance\n- **Cardiovascular benefits:** CPAP reduces risk of hypertension, atrial fibrillation, stroke, and myocardial infarction in severe OSA",
