# MCQ 154: Ventilator Settings Basics
MCQ_154 = {
    "question_id": "WEEK3_RESP_154",
    "topic": "Ventilation & Pleural Disease",
    "subtopic": "Mechanical Ventilation - Basic Settings",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "A 65-year-old male (height 175 cm, actual weight 80 kg, ideal body weight 70 kg) is intubated in the Intensive Care Unit for acute hypoxaemic respiratory failure secondary to community-acquired pneumonia. The treating team initiates volume-controlled ventilation. Which initial ventilator settings are MOST appropriate according to lung-protective ventilation principles?",
    "options": {
        "A": "Tidal volume 800 mL (10 mL/kg actual weight), PEEP 5 cmH2O, FiO2 0.6, respiratory rate 12/min",
        "B": "Tidal volume 560 mL (8 mL/kg actual weight), PEEP 8 cmH2O, FiO2 0.6, respiratory rate 14/min",
        "C": "Tidal volume 420 mL (6 mL/kg ideal body weight), PEEP 8 cmH2O, FiO2 0.6, respiratory rate 16/min",
        "D": "Tidal volume 350 mL (5 mL/kg ideal body weight), PEEP 12 cmH2O, FiO2 1.0, respiratory rate 20/min",
        "E": "Tidal volume 700 mL (10 mL/kg ideal body weight), PEEP 5 cmH2O, FiO2 0.4, respiratory rate 10/min"
    },
    "correct_answer": "C",
    "explanation": "Lung-protective ventilation is the evidence-based standard of care for all mechanically ventilated patients, particularly those with acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and acute hypoxaemic respiratory failure. Option C reflects current Australian and international guidelines.\n\n**Why Option C is correct:**\n- **Tidal volume calculation:** Low tidal volume (VT) strategy uses 6-8 mL/kg of ideal body weight (IBW), NOT actual weight. For this patient: IBW (male) = 50 kg + 0.91 × (height in cm - 152.4) = 50 + 0.91 × (175 - 152.4) = 50 + 20.5 = 70.5 kg ≈ 70 kg. Target VT = 6 mL/kg IBW = 6 × 70 = 420 mL. This matches Option C\n- **PEEP 8 cmH2O:** Appropriate starting PEEP for acute hypoxaemic respiratory failure. Higher PEEP (5-10 cmH2O) recruits atelectatic alveoli, improves oxygenation, and prevents cyclic alveolar collapse/reopening (atelectrauma). The ARDSNet PEEP/FiO2 table recommends PEEP 8-10 cmH2O for FiO2 0.6\n- **FiO2 0.6:** Acceptable starting FiO2 for acute hypoxaemia. Should be titrated down to maintain SpO2 88-95% (or PaO2 55-80 mmHg) to minimize oxygen toxicity. FiO2 >0.6 for >24 hours increases risk of absorption atelectasis and oxygen-induced lung injury\n- **Respiratory rate 16/min:** Adequate to maintain minute ventilation (VT × RR = 420 × 16 = 6.7 L/min) and prevent hypercapnia while using low tidal volumes. May need adjustment based on arterial blood gas\n\n**Lung-protective ventilation principles (ARDSNet protocol):**\n1. **Low tidal volume:** 6 mL/kg IBW (range 4-8 mL/kg). Reduces volutrauma and ventilator-induced lung injury (VILI)\n2. **Plateau pressure limit:** Target plateau pressure (Pplat) ≤30 cmH2O. High plateau pressures cause alveolar overdistension and barotrauma\n3. **Permissive hypercapnia:** Accept PaCO2 up to 60-80 mmHg (pH >7.20) to maintain low VT. Contraindications: raised intracranial pressure, severe metabolic acidosis\n4. **PEEP optimization:** PEEP 5-15 cmH2O to prevent atelectasis and improve oxygenation. Use ARDSNet PEEP/FiO2 tables\n5. **FiO2 minimization:** Titrate to lowest FiO2 maintaining SpO2 88-95% to avoid oxygen toxicity\n\n**Why other options are incorrect:**\n- **Option A (VT 10 mL/kg actual weight):** Traditional 'historical' ventilation using 10-12 mL/kg is HARMFUL. The landmark ARDSNet trial (2000) showed high VT (12 mL/kg) increased mortality by 22% compared to low VT (6 mL/kg) in ARDS. Additionally, this uses actual weight (incorrect for VT calculation - must use IBW to avoid excessive VT in obese/overweight patients). VT 800 mL for this patient would cause volutrauma\n- **Option B (VT 8 mL/kg actual weight):** While 8 mL/kg is upper limit of lung-protective range, it should be based on IBW not actual weight. 560 mL exceeds 8 mL/kg IBW (8 × 70 = 560 mL, but patient's actual weight calculation gives wrong basis). Using actual weight in obese patients causes excessive VT and VILI. The error in methodology makes this incorrect\n- **Option D (VT 5 mL/kg IBW):** While ultra-low VT (4-5 mL/kg) may be used in severe ARDS with high plateau pressures, it's not standard initial setting. VT 350 mL would require very high respiratory rates (>20/min) to maintain minute ventilation, increasing risk of air trapping and auto-PEEP. PEEP 12 cmH2O is high for initial settings (risk of haemodynamic compromise). FiO2 1.0 is excessive and increases oxygen toxicity risk - should start lower and titrate\n- **Option E (VT 10 mL/kg IBW):** VT 700 mL (10 mL/kg IBW) is excessive and violates lung-protective principles. This will cause volutrauma. Additionally, RR 10/min is too low (minute ventilation only 7 L/min) and may cause hypercapnia. PEEP 5 cmH2O is at lower limit for acute hypoxaemic failure\n\n**Australian Context:**\n- **ANZICS guidelines:** Recommend lung-protective ventilation (VT 6-8 mL/kg IBW, Pplat ≤30 cmH2O) for all mechanically ventilated ICU patients, not just ARDS\n- **ICU outcome data:** Australian ICU databases show implementation of lung-protective ventilation reduced ventilator-associated pneumonia rates by 30% and ICU length of stay by 1.5 days\n- **eTG Complete:** Endorses ARDSNet protocol for acute hypoxaemic respiratory failure and ARDS\n\n**Clinical Pearls:**\n- **IBW calculation:** Males: 50 + 0.91 × (height in cm - 152.4). Females: 45.5 + 0.91 × (height in cm - 152.4)\n- **Plateau pressure measurement:** Perform inspiratory hold manoeuvre (0.5-second pause at end-inspiration). Pplat reflects alveolar pressure (vs peak pressure which includes airway resistance). Target Pplat ≤30 cmH2O\n- **Driving pressure:** Pplat - PEEP. Strong predictor of mortality in ARDS. Target driving pressure <15 cmH2O\n- **Permissive hypercapnia:** If pH drops <7.20 with low VT, consider sodium bicarbonate infusion or cautiously increase VT to 7-8 mL/kg (max Pplat still ≤30 cmH2O)\n- **Monitoring:** ABG 30 min after ventilator changes, then 4-hourly. Daily CXR. Plateau pressure checks every 4 hours",
    "citations": [
        {
            "source": "ARDSNet ARMA Trial (2000)",
            "guidance": "Landmark trial showing low tidal volume ventilation (6 mL/kg IBW) reduced mortality by 22% (31% to 39.8%, p=0.007) compared to traditional high tidal volume (12 mL/kg IBW) in ARDS patients. Plateau pressure limited to ≤30 cmH2O",
            "access_date": "2000",
